\begin{abstract}
This journal article presents a unified mathematical formulation of the National
Infrastructure Simulation Stress Test Protocol. The protocol consists of four
execution phases: ingest saturation, thermodynamic substrate shock, sovereign
kernel fault injection, and zero-knowledge audit. Each phase contributes a
distinct operator to the evolution of the encrypted state tensor $\Psi(x,t)$.
We derive the governing equations, stability metrics, invariant conditions,
and cryptographic--astrophysical coupling terms relevant to the sovereign
simulation architecture.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Large-scale national infrastructure simulations require robust stress testing
under extreme ingest rates, thermodynamic perturbations, cryptographic load,
and autonomous kernel recovery conditions. This work formalizes the mathematical
structure of the stress-test protocol and provides a unified evolution equation
for the encrypted state tensor $\Psi(x,t)$.

